1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:07,820
Welcome back to today's deep dive, where, uh, our mission is really understanding how to build foolproof data quality checks into your digital systems.

2
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:13,640
Exactly, yeah. Because, I mean, imagine you are managing a massive government database in your ministry, right?

3
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:16,219
And bad data just keeps slipping in.

4
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:18,400
It's a nightmare. It just, uh,

5
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:20,400
infects everything downstream.

6
00:00:20,940 --> 00:00:30,920
Right. Which is why today we are unpacking exactly how you can catch those bad data rows before they contaminate your entire system, and importantly, without leaving your users completely

7
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:31,320
in the dark.

8
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:34,260
So to start, let's look at a practical scenario.

9
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:41,760
Say a head teacher submits a terms list of learners to your program, but, you know, three of those rows are just completely wrong.

10
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:44,920
Yeah. That creates a major trap for the whole system.

11
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:50,460
How so? Well, if the database just accepts them, every connected service inherits the mistakes.

12
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:55,640
But if it drops them silently, the school assumes everything went through fine, so the errors just sit there.

13
00:00:56,180 --> 00:00:58,580
They stay fragmented and inconsistent, basically.

14
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:03,160
Exactly. They get duplicated, rekeyed, and become totally out of step with reality.

15
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:08,500
So wait, why not just run checks on the data the absolute millisecond it hits the server?

16
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:09,960
Just block it at the gate.

17
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:13,360
Because doing that can actually crash your entire ingestion pipeline.

18
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:18,640
I mean, if a file is completely malformed, running complex checks immediately might just break the system.

19
00:01:18,860 --> 00:01:20,320
Oh, wow. I, I wouldn't have thought of that.

20
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:23,980
Yeah, so in the reference architecture, raw data is actually never checked.

21
00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:28,100
It just lands. The checks only run at what is called the bronze tier.

22
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:33,600
The bronze tier. Okay, so that's like the very first internal sorting area, right?

23
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:36,260
Before anyone actually approves the data for use.

24
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:42,220
Correct. You are applying rigorous, predictable structure before that data comes anywhere near the main register.

25
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:46,360
Right. And Giga actually has a published data flow that handles this perfectly, right?

26
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:55,100
They do, yeah. It runs checks at that bronze tier and actively splits the incoming rows into two separate tables: one for rows that passed and one for rows that failed.

27
00:01:55,500 --> 00:02:02,480
And they use the GovStack digital registry specification for that, which, uh, basically provides standard digital blueprints for governments.

28
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:07,280
Exactly. It allows the system to set validation rules for every single field.

29
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:12,960
Things like, you know, requiring a value, ensuring it is unique, or setting minimum and maximum limits.

30
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:17,180
So what do these failures actually look like in practice?

31
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:21,180
Let's use, uh, Progressa, the fictional country from the examples.

32
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:25,920
Sure. So Progressa catches three faulty school rows at the bronze tier.

33
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:30,740
In the first row, the same learner appears twice, so it's a duplicate learner number.

34
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:32,280
Okay, that's a clear error.

35
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:36,900
Right. In the second, a required field, the date of birth, is just completely empty.

36
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:41,840
And in the third row, the date of birth claims a three-year-old child is in grade six.

37
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:45,920
Okay, wait. I have to ask, why are we talking about policy here?

38
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:50,640
Can't the IT guys just write a quick script that flags anyone under ten in the sixth grade?

39
00:02:51,220 --> 00:02:54,340
It is incredibly dangerous to leave that to the IT department.

40
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:55,760
Really? Why?

41
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:02,400
Because setting limits, like the age range for a specific grade, is fundamentally a policy choice.

42
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:04,900
It has to be made by the register's owner.

43
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:09,440
Ah, I see. So the technology only enforces the rules.

44
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:13,240
Exactly. The policy dictates what those rules actually are.

45
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:18,740
I mean, if a programmer is just guessing the rules, they are essentially writing your minister's policy in code.

46
00:03:19,240 --> 00:03:24,120
That makes total sense. So going back to the data, what happens to those three failed rows?

47
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:26,820
Do we just delete them to save server space?

48
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:31,120
No, they are actually kept alongside plain text reasons for their failure.

49
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:32,540
Oh, interesting. In-

50
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:33,560
So they aren't just tossed out.

51
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:43,760
Right. In Giga's flow, a data quality report is generated and emailed straight back to the sender, like the head teacher, so they get a short list showing exactly which rows failed and what they need

52
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:44,240
to correct.

53
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:48,400
Which means a successful system walkthrough is highly predictable, right?

54
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:51,340
Very predictable. There are three clear steps.

55
00:03:51,720 --> 00:03:59,220
Step one, you use a seeded load containing those three faulty rows we mentioned: the duplicate, the missing required value, and the out-of-range value.

56
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:01,640
Right, the three-year-old in sixth grade.

57
00:04:01,940 --> 00:04:10,360
Exactly. Then step two, a good run passes only when each of those three lands exclusively in the failed table with its specific reasons stated.

58
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:11,300
And step three?

59
00:04:11,780 --> 00:04:15,320
Step three is that absolutely no faulty row ever reaches staging.

60
00:04:15,820 --> 00:04:20,820
Staging being, of course, the final waiting room before data goes live into the main production database.

61
00:04:21,279 --> 00:04:21,760
You got it.

62
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:22,680
So

63
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:26,000
synthesizing all of this for everyone listening,

64
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:29,280
you want to check every row at the bronze tier,

65
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:32,380
set the failing rows aside with plain text reasons,

66
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,780
and send the sender a clear list of what to correct.

67
00:04:36,220 --> 00:04:45,900
That is the core lesson, yeah. And by reporting the share of failed rows term by term to your director, you aren't just cleaning up a database, you are actively training your users and

68
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:48,880
transforming the data culture of your entire organization.

69
00:04:49,380 --> 00:04:51,700
It's a fundamental shift in how you handle information.

70
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:52,740
It really is.

71
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:55,300
And think about the stakes if you get this wrong.

72
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:59,300
We're talking about the logic that governs modern societies.

73
00:04:59,920 --> 00:05:01,860
Right, because if bad data gets through...

74
00:05:02,440 --> 00:05:07,500
Or if your rejection rules are flawed and just silently drop rows without feedback-

75
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:12,120
You could have automated systems permanently locking citizens out of essential public services.

76
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:14,760
Wow. Like a child missing out on school funding.

77
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:15,500
Exactly.

78
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:22,840
Or a family being denied healthcare simply because a silent data error threw their record out and nobody ever told them how to fix it.
